+ # CertGate **CertGate prepares the review of a steel mill inspection certificate against a purchase specification, and proposes release, block or review. A person in Quality decides.** CertGate never releases material on its own. > **All data in this repository is fictitious.** Siderúrgica Valdemora S.A. (the mill), Talleres Ribera Norte > S.L. (the buyer), the grade TRN-355 and the purchase specification ESP-014 are invented for the demo. The limits > in ESP-014 belong to that fictitious company and are **not** the limits of any real standard. Only these > synthetic certificates are ever sent to the model endpoint. Companion documents: [METRICS.md](METRICS.md) (evaluation, generated) · [DEPLOY.md](DEPLOY.md) (Vercel, for the human) · [CLAIMS.md](CLAIMS.md) (every capability sentence and its proof) · [EVIDENCE-INDEX.md](EVIDENCE-INDEX.md) (where the evidence is). ## Problem and user When a batch of steel arrives at a plant, it comes with a mill certificate (an inspection certificate of the EN 10204 3.1 kind): heat or lot, grade, chemical composition and measured mechanical properties. Someone in goods-in or Quality must check that the certificate belongs to that batch and that the values meet the purchase specification. Today this is done by hand, with certificates in different layouts and sometimes scanned. Material out of spec that reaches production means scrap, claims or a recall. - **Primary user:** goods-in or Quality technician at a mid-size plant that buys certified material. - **Who decides:** the Quality manager who signs the release or the block. ## What it does For each case (certificate PDF plus its delivery-note line), CertGate: 1. Validates the files (PDF by magic bytes, size limits, not encrypted, has pages) and records their SHA-256. 2. Reads the certificate through two channels: - the **model channel**: the open model Qwen3.8-27B, served by AMD Token Factory, reads the rendered **page images** of every certificate, digital or scanned, and returns the values as one JSON object; - the **text layer** of a digital PDF, extracted without any model. A scan has no text layer, so its critical fields need human confirmation, and CertGate says so. 3. Compares the channels. A disagreement makes the field unknown and the case goes to review. 4. Checks internal coherence: heat or lot against the delivery note, expected units and scale jumps, and the printed carbon equivalent against one recomputed from the composition. The formula's source is cited in [docs/sources/cev-formula.md](docs/sources/cev-formula.md). 5. Applies deterministic, versioned rules against the spec, with exact decimals (never binary floating point). 6. Proposes `release`, `block` or `review`, records the person's decision (who, when, why) and exports a run receipt without secrets. Proposal precedence: a heat mismatch means review; otherwise block > review > release. Release is proposed only when no review or block condition holds. A scan is never proposed for release until a person has confirmed every critical field. ## What runs on AMD, and how it is shown - **Endpoint:** AMD Token Factory, the AMD compute of the event (OpenAI-compatible; host `developer.amd.com.cn`). - **Model:** requested as `Qwen3.8-27B`; every reply reports it as `self-dploy/Qwen3.8-27B`. Its publisher is the Qwen Team, and its licence, as published by the publisher, is apache-2.0 (https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B, retrieved 2026-10-13; see [docs/sources/model.md](docs/sources/model.md)). - **What the model does:** it reads the page images of a certificate into the CertGate reading contract: value, unit, status, and the location where it says it found the value. Requests use temperature 0 and a frozen instruction (SHA-256 `32fad2f9…`). - **What the model never does:** it never decides and never explains. The text-layer comparison, the coherence checks, the rules and the proposal run in the CertGate backend without any model, and a person decides. The model's reasoning text is never used or stored. - **How it is shown:** - The "Runs on AMD" panel (`#/amd`) shows only what the endpoint reports: the model list, the key's rate limit and daily spend, and the latency, tokens and cost of the last reading. - Every receipt of a real reading (live or recorded) carries a copy of those values from reading time, plus the endpoint reply's id, created time, model and usage. - The endpoint does not expose the GPU model, the ROCm version or the serving-stack version. The panel says "not exposed by the endpoint" for them, and nothing else claims them. - **Cost:** the cost shown is the cost reported by the endpoint; Token Factory credits are free and have no monetary value. ## Evaluation (real model) Two independent real runs read the same 27 fictitious certificates (cg-004 … cg-030). cg-001 … cg-003 were used to tune the prompt and are excluded. Every figure comes from the committed run artifacts. The full tables, the reporting rules (FC-broad and FC-strict, rule abstention versus failure, confident-on-illegible, aggregates over processed cases, usage reconciliation) and the per-case comparison of the two runs are in [METRICS.md](METRICS.md), which is generated from those artifacts. | | Implementer run | Auditor's independent run | |---|---|---| | Artifacts | [evaluation/runs/real-candidate-dd557fd/](evaluation/runs/real-candidate-dd557fd/REPORT.md) | [stage-3/auditor/candidate-3/real-run/eval-retry1/](evidence/runs/certgate-001/stage-3/auditor/candidate-3/real-run/eval-retry1/) | | Candidate · time (UTC) | `dd557fd` · 2026-10-13 17:28–17:49 | `a892f771` · 2026-10-13 19:13–19:31 | | **False conforming** (released when the expected proposal was not release) | **0 of 18** (strict, expected block: 0 of 6) | **0 of 18** (strict: 0 of 6) | | Non-conformities caught | 6 of 6, all proposed block | 6 of 6, all proposed block | | Proposal as expected | 27 of 27 | 26 of 27 (cg-004: review, expected release) | | Failures · retries | 0 of 27 · 0 | 0 of 27 · 0 | | Field readings correct | 591 of 594 | 591 of 594 | | Seconds per certificate | mean 89.6, median 80.6 (N 27) | mean 77.1, median 76.9 (N 27) | | Cost per certificate | mean 0.00020529 | mean 0.00020863 | - Costs are the cost reported by the endpoint; Token Factory credits are free and have no monetary value. - The reading path, the prompt and the rules are identical by diff in both candidates and in the accepted candidate `5c904693`. - The landing page shows the figures of the implementer run and names that run. They come from `public/figures.json`, which is generated from the artifacts and never written by hand. ## Demo procedure Start the container in mock mode (no endpoint and no network needed); see "Run it". Then open `http://localhost:3000/` in a browser: 1. **Landing:** the problem in one sentence, the four evaluation figures with their denominators, and the fictitious-data notice. 2. **Judge mode, three recorded real runs:** cg-001 (conforming, release proposed), cg-002 (scan out of spec, block) and cg-003 (ambiguous scan, block). Each card is labelled "recorded real reading … not live" with its recording date. Judge mode makes no model call and has no limit. 3. **cg-003, the case it refuses to release:** a scan has no second reading channel, so release stays disabled. Confirm every critical field by hand with ReH 358 (the true value) and re-run the rules: release becomes possible, and the person records the decision with a reason. With ReH 338, the proposal is block. 4. **Open the receipt:** it shows the values, both channels, the rules, the endpoint data at reading time, and a JSON download. 5. **Runs on AMD (`#/amd`):** without an endpoint it says "endpoint off" with the time of the check. 6. **Live reading (container in real mode only):** start the container with the three `MODEL_*` variables (see "Run it"), choose a dataset certificate, confirm the third-party notice, and press "Read live". A reading takes about 1 to 4 minutes. ## Run it Requirements: Node.js 24 (local run) or Docker. ```sh # Build the image docker build --no-cache -t certgate . # Mock mode: the web UI at http://localhost:3000 (no endpoint: judge mode shows the recorded real runs; "endpoint off") docker run --rm -d --name certgate-ui -p 3000:3000 -e PORT=3000 certgate docker stop certgate-ui # The service on PORT, reachable from the host (a published port needs a network; the service itself makes # no outbound call in mock mode) docker run --rm -d --name certgate -p 3000:3000 -e PORT=3000 certgate curl -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/cases/cg-003/process docker stop certgate # The service fully offline: with --network none no port can be published, so query it from inside docker run --rm --network none -e PORT=3000 certgate sh -c 'node dist/src/server.js & sleep 2; node -e "fetch(\"http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/cases/cg-003/process\",{method:\"POST\"}).then(r=>r.text()).then(console.log)"' # One dataset case through the CLI, inside the image docker run --rm --network none certgate node dist/src/cli.js process --case cg-003 # The test suite inside the image (makes no endpoint call; the checks over the delivery documents and evidence, # test/docs.test.ts and test/metrics-md.test.ts, are skipped there because the image carries neither) docker run --rm --network none certgate npx vitest run # Local npm ci npm run build npm test node dist/src/cli.js list node dist/src/cli.js process --case cg-001 ``` ### Real mode: live reading against AMD Token Factory (container or local) Live reading runs from the container, not from the deployed site. ```sh # MODEL_BASE_URL, MODEL_API_KEY and MODEL_ID come from your environment; never write their values anywhere. docker run --rm -p 3000:3000 -e PORT=3000 -e MODEL_BASE_URL -e MODEL_API_KEY -e MODEL_ID -e LIVE_READS_PER_HOUR=20 certgate ``` With these set, the "Runs on AMD" panel reads the endpoint, and "Read live" can read a dataset certificate. A visitor-uploaded file also needs `LIVE_ACCESS_CODE` to be configured and given. ### CLI ``` certgate list certgate process --case <id> dataset case data/cases/<id> certgate process --case-dir <dir> [--line <n>] [--delivery <csv>] <dir>/certificate.pdf, <dir>/reading.json (mock model output), <dir>/delivery.csv (or --delivery); the paired delivery line from --line or <dir>/case.json "delivery_line" options: --out <file> also write the output to a file --receipt-only print only the receipt JSON --confirm <field>=<value> human confirmation of a critical field (repeatable; value "absent" = not on document) --confirmed-by <name> --decide <release|block|request_new_certificate> --reason <text> --decided-by <name> exit codes: 0 processed (any proposal, including reader errors), 2 input rejected, 1 internal error ``` The CLI reads dataset cases through the offline `MockReader`, which returns the dataset's prepared reading and is labelled `MOCK` in every output. With `CERTGATE_MODE=real` the CLI has no adapter and fails visibly; it never falls back to the mock. Field names for `--confirm`: `certificate_type`, `heat_or_lot`, `grade`, `chemistry.<C|Si|Mn|P|S|Cr|Ni|Mo|Cu|V>`, `cev_printed`, `mechanical.<ReH|Rm|A|KV_1|KV_2|KV_3>`, `kv_temperature`, `inspection_stamp`. A confirmation without a unit is taken in the field's expected unit. ### Web UI and HTTP API Open `http://localhost:<PORT>/`. Screens: - landing and judge mode (`#/`); - a recorded run (`#/story/cg-003`); - a case kept in this browser (`#/local/<id>`); - the "Runs on AMD" panel (`#/amd`); - each case's receipt, with a JSON download. Every page carries the fictitious-data notice. Pages make no third-party request. | Method and path | Result | |---|---| | `GET /health` | status and mode | | `GET /api/f1` | "Runs on AMD" data from the configured endpoint, probed at most once per 60 s (`no-store`) | | `GET /api/live/status` | live reading: enabled, limit, remaining this hour, reopening time, windows, upload policy, notice (`no-store`) | | `POST /api/live/read` | `{certificate_base64, delivery_csv?, delivery_line?, access_code?, synthetic_confirmed: true}`: a live reading, or C11 `endpoint_off` with no endpoint (`no-store`) | | `POST /api/stories/<cg-001\|cg-002\|cg-003>/evaluate` | `{confirmations?: [{field, value}], confirmed_by?, decision?: {action, reason, decided_by}}`: the recorded reading re-evaluated (no model call) with a person's confirmations and decision; times are stamped by the server | | `GET /stories/<id>.json`, `GET /stories/<id>.recording.json` | recorded run and its stored endpoint reply (versioned static files) | | `GET /figures.json` | landing figures, generated from the evaluation artifacts | | `GET /api/cases`, `POST /api/cases/<id>/process` | Stage 1 dataset API (mock path) | Release is rejected (HTTP 409, and in the core) while the proposal is not release. `GET /health` reports two separate fields. `mode` is the mode of the CLI and the dataset API (`CERTGATE_MODE`, `mock` by default). `reader` is `live` when an endpoint and a model are configured outside the deployed site, and `off` otherwise. A container in real mode therefore answers `"mode": "mock", "reader": "live"`: the dataset API still uses the mock, and live reading uses the endpoint. ### Live reading against the local test stub (tests and review only) ```sh node test/support/stub-endpoint.mjs --port 8001 # TEST STUB, not a model; --mode timeout|http_429|http_5xx|invalid_json|huge MODEL_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8001/v1 MODEL_ID=certgate-test-stub MODEL_API_KEY=stub-key \ LIVE_READS_PER_HOUR=3 PORT=3000 node dist/src/server.js ``` ### Evaluation, metrics and stories `npx vitest run` makes no endpoint call and passes with `--network none`. The live reader is tested against replies recorded from the endpoint (`test/fixtures/endpoint/`) and against a local stub. The commands that call the endpoint are separate and need the three `MODEL_*` variables: ```sh npm run build node tools/evaluate/run-eval.mjs --out evaluation/runs/<name> # endpoint: cg-004..cg-030, GET /usage before and after node dist/src/tools/build-stories.js record # endpoint: re-record the three stories (3 readings) node tools/evaluate/metrics.mjs evaluation/runs/<name> # offline: recompute metrics.json from results.json node tools/evaluate/figures.mjs evaluation/runs/<name> # offline: regenerate public/figures.json node tools/evaluate/metrics-md.mjs # offline: regenerate METRICS.md (--check to compare) node dist/src/tools/build-stories.js # offline: rebuild the stories from the recordings ``` Browser checks (need Playwright with Chromium, not part of `npm test`): `node test/browser/ui-checks.mjs [outDir]`. ### Configuration Environment variables only; see [.env.example](.env.example), which has no values (`.env` is ignored by git). Secrets (`MODEL_API_KEY`, `LIVE_ACCESS_CODE`) stay on the server and are never sent to the browser. | Variable | Default | Meaning | |---|---|---| | `CERTGATE_MODE` | `mock` | CLI and dataset API: `mock` (offline, dataset readings) or `real` (no adapter: fails visibly) | | `PORT` | `3000` | HTTP port | | `CERTGATE_DATA_DIR` | `<repo>/data` | dataset directory | | `MODEL_BASE_URL` | none | OpenAI-compatible endpoint (AMD Token Factory), including `/v1`; unset = endpoint off | | `MODEL_API_KEY` | none | secret, sent only to that endpoint | | `MODEL_ID` | none | model id requested from the endpoint | | `LIVE_READS_PER_HOUR` | none (disabled) | live readings per UTC hour, server-side, per instance; unset, invalid or 0 = disabled; documented deployment value 20 | | `LIVE_ACCESS_CODE` | none (no default) | needed for live reading of a visitor-uploaded file; without it only dataset certificates are read live | | `LIVE_CODE_FAILURES_PER_HOUR` | `5` | wrong codes allowed per UTC hour before rejection | | `LIVE_WINDOWS` | none | announced live windows, shown as text | | `VERCEL` | set by Vercel | on the deployed site live reading is unavailable (time budget, see Limitations) | Input limits: - CLI and container: certificate PDF up to 10 MiB; delivery note up to 1 MiB. - Web upload: **up to 3 MiB** of PDF, sent as base64 so the request stays below Vercel's 4.5 MB body limit; delivery note up to 256 KiB. Sources: [docs/sources/vercel-limits.md](docs/sources/vercel-limits.md). ## Limitations - **Shared service.** AMD Token Factory is a shared service. - Per key: about 20–30 requests per minute, 8 concurrent requests, and a rolling daily spend cap read from GET /usage (1, in free credits). - During the human's test it answered 429 (concurrency), 504 from the upstream, and connection timeouts. In the band's two evaluation runs there were 0 retries and 0 failures, and every proposal was as expected. - CertGate keeps at most 2 requests in flight, retries only 429 and 5xx (at most 3 attempts, Retry-After honoured), and waits up to 300 s per attempt. - The provider keeps prompts and outputs for about 2 days, so only synthetic data may be sent. Before any live reading the page shows that notice, and the server requires the visitor to confirm it. - **Slow readings.** One reading takes about 1 to 4 minutes (52–208 s per certificate in the evaluation). - **Deployed site has no live reading (OI-S2-02).** - The worst case of one live reading is 3 × 300 s + 2 × 120 s = 1140 s, above the 300 s Vercel function duration. - With `VERCEL` set, `/api/live/read` answers 503 "live reading unavailable on the deployed site; live demonstration from the container", and the page says so. - Judge mode works on the deployed site; live reading is demonstrated from the container. - **Live-read limit per instance (OI-S2-01).** The limit is counted on the server, per server instance, in memory. Measured consequence: two instances with a limit of 2 let 4 readings through (test "the live limit is per instance" in `test/web.test.ts`). With N instances, the real ceiling is N × `LIVE_READS_PER_HOUR` per hour, all drawn on the same endpoint key. A shared store behind the same interface (`src/web/limiter.ts`) would make it global. - **Uploads.** Without `LIVE_ACCESS_CODE`, live reading is limited to the 30 dataset certificates, matched by SHA-256; with it, a visitor's synthetic file can also be read. The access-code path is tested against a local stub with a test-only code, and no real access code is configured on the band's host. - **cg-003, the ambiguous scan.** The model reads the smudged yield strength confidently as **338** MPa; the true value is 358. It did so in both completed tuning reads, in the recorded story and in the adversary's probe. The product therefore proposes **block** (rule failure plus no second channel), never release, and the scan still needs a person to confirm every critical field. The story card shows what the recorded run shows. The prompt was not tuned to change this. - **Decimal comma, no unit.** The model sometimes returns a printed CEV with a decimal comma and no unit (`0,41`). It happened once in each real run, on a different case each time (cg-028 in the implementer run, cg-004 in the auditor's). The text-layer channel check caught it every time, and the case went to review. - **No model-written explanation.** The model is not asked for an explanation, and the product never shows one. This deviates from step 7 of the product design (03-DISENO); it was chosen to keep decisions entirely in the deterministic rules (I1). The case file shows the rule reasons. - **Locations are the model's claim.** The page and fragment where the model says it found a value are labelled "as reported by the model" and are not verified. On digital PDFs the text layer checks the values, not the locations. - **Hostile duplicate field.** A text layer with a spoofed duplicate of a field (cg-022: "ReH (MPa) 400" while the table says 340) makes that field unknown. A would-be block becomes review, never release. - **Prompt examples.** The reading instruction's examples reuse values of cg-001 ("0.16", "392", "QA-07"). cg-001 is a tuning and story case, outside the 27-case evaluation. - **Text-layer parser.** It covers the two fictitious certificate layouts of the dataset, not arbitrary mill certificates. - **Cosmetic:** `/favicon.ico` answers 404. - **One endpoint and one model.** The results apply to Qwen3.8-27B on AMD Token Factory, as reported on 2026-10-13. A repeat run may differ: the two independent runs differ in one proposal (cg-004, review) and in timing. ## Dataset The dataset has 30 fictitious certificates with planted faults, 25 digital and 5 scanned. All are fictitious: three demo certificates were created before the kickoff, and 27 were created in this run. The expected outcomes were written and committed before any product code. For the cases whose planted fault exists only in the mock, the real-model expectations were written by hand before the first real run ([evaluation/expectations-real-run.json](evaluation/expectations-real-run.json)). See [data/README.md](data/README.md) for provenance, coverage and checksums. ## Reason codes The codes of [data/expected/README.md](data/expected/README.md), plus these, raised by the product: | Code | When | Effect | |---|---|---| | `TEXT_LAYER_MISSING:<field>` | The model channel read a field that the text layer does not contain | review | | `RULE_NOT_EVALUABLE:<field>` | A spec rule's input is unknown | review | | `NOT_A_NUMBER:<field>` | A numeric field holds text that is not a decimal | review | | `READER_ERROR:invalid_output` | Model output parses as JSON but breaks the reading schema | review, status error | | `READER_ERROR:endpoint_not_configured` | `CERTGATE_MODE=real` without an adapter | review, status error | ## Repository layout ``` src/ product code (TypeScript, Node 24) public/ web UI, recorded stories, landing figures api/ Vercel function entry test/ tests (vitest), runnable offline; test/browser: Playwright checks config/ band-defined plausibility ranges (not from any standard) data/ fictitious dataset, spec ESP-014 r3, delivery notes docs/ cited sources evaluation/ real-run expectations, tuning exclusions, development call log, evaluation runs tools/ dataset generator (Python, dev-time), evaluation, metrics and development tools evidence/ stage records of the band (registers, handoffs, verdicts, logs) ``` ## How it was built CertGate was built by a band of four Claude Code seats: analyst, implementer, adversary and auditor. - The implementer alone wrote the product. - The adversary attacked it, including mutation checks. - The auditor accepted or rejected each stage independently. In Stage 3 it made its own real evaluation run. The UI was designed with Claude Design and implemented by the band: the design defines the look, and the behaviour follows the written specification. The model that reads the certificates in this build runs on AMD Token Factory. ## License [MIT](LICENSE). 

